## Background and Originality Content
Global warming is one of our great concerns due to the rapid growth of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ emission.[1] To reduce the anthropogenic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ emission, great efforts have been devoted to the development of diverse ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture materials and many porous solids have been intensively investigated, such as zeolites, carbons, etc.[2] Compared with traditional porous materials (zeolite, active carbon, and others), metal-organic frameworks (MOFs),[3] due to their intriguing and controllable structures, are regarded as a promising kind of material for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture.[3b,3f,4] Very recently, much work is focused on three dimensional (3D) MOFs such as MOF74-Mg,[5] SIFSIX-3-Zn,[2a] and NJU-Bai7.[6] In contrast, for the two dimensional (2D) MOFs, seldom successful examples were reported on improving the selective adsorption of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . As we all know, the layers of 2D MOF are held together depending on the packing mode, which is composed by different interactions between the adjacent layers.[7] Very interestingly, the layers could slide smoothly by external stimuli. Therefore, 2D MOFs have the potential to provide dynamic porous materials.
The first 2D MOF (MOF-2) based upon $^{1,4}$ -dicarboxylate bentypical MOFs have been constructed by many groups.[7e-n] Among zene was discovered by Yaghi’s group ,[7d] and later a series of them, Zaworotko’s group had studied the construction of novel 2D MOFs based on 1,3-benzenedicarboxylate and pyridine ligands. Besides, they explored the concept of self-assembly of nanoscale secondary building units for 2D MOFs.[7i] Fujita’s group studies the construction of novel 2D MOFs based on pyridine ligands as well as the dynamic property of 2D coordination layers triggered by the guest exchange.[7f,8] However, there are very few studies on 2D MOFs to improve the selective adsorption of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ .
Very recently, one of our research focuses is the construction of novel MOFs with potential applications in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture from bitopic ligand, which is a pyridylcarboxylate ligand.[6,9] For example, based on the organic ligand of isonicotinic acid (HIN), through the approach of finely tuning multinuclear clusters of MOFs, bcu-type NJU-Bai35 with highly selective ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ was prepared.[10] To further enrich the strategy of finely tuning structure of MOFs, we chose this simple bitopic ligand to construct two new 2D MOFs, one of which has highly selective ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake.
## Results and Discussion
Herein, we present the synthesis, structural characterization, and gas sorption isotherm measurements for the microporous framework of crystalline $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{H}|\mathsf{N})_{4}(\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{C}00)_{2}]_{n}$ (CuHIN). Furthermore, we compare the gas sorption properties between MOF-2 and CuHIN based upon the packing mode, which causes the sliding of the adjacent layers. Solvothermal reaction of $\mathsf{C u C l}_{2}{\cdot}2\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ with HIN in ${\sf D M F}/{\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}$ containing $C H_{3}C O O H$ afforded green block crystals of CuHIN. Single X-ray crystal structure revealed that it crystallizes in monoclinic space group $\textsf{P}21/c$ . In the structure of CuHIN, Cu-paddlewheel units $[\mathsf{C u}_{2}(\mathsf{C O O})_{4}\mathsf{N}_{2}]$ are linked by two pyridyl N atoms from two independent HIN ligands, and four carboxylates from two HIN ligands and two acetic acids, leading to the formation of 2D square topological layers (sql) with a pore size of $4.0\times4.0\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{2}$ (Figures 1a and 1b). These layers are further pillared along the $c$ axis by the $\pi^{\cdot\cdot\cdot\pi}$ interactions of the pyridine rings in the adjacent layers, and the paddlewheel is held in the cavity by interactions with the $\pi^{\cdot\cdot\cdot\pi}$ resulting in an AB packing mode of 3D framework (Figures 2a and 2b). For the MOF-2, the structure extends into the (011) crystallographic plane by having identical Zn-Zn units linked to carboxylate functionalities of BDC to yield 2-D microporous layers. These layers are held together along the $a$ axis by hydrogen-bonding interactions between water from the paddlewheel of one layer and carboxylate oxygens of an adjacent layer, which results in an AA packing mode of 3D framework. Stacking of the layers in the interaction with the water ligands, prevents the paddlewheel holding in the cavity by van der Waals interactions with the $Z n(B D C)$ framework. Compared with the structure of MOF-2, the 2D nets in CuHIN with AB packing mode have weak interaction between pyridine rings in the adjacent layers, which may be easily triggered by external stimuli, and might result in dynamic channel dimension smaller than $4.0\times$ $4.0\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{2}$ along the $c$ axis (more approximately to the kinetic diameter of $C O_{2}^{\cdot}$ . Moreover, the total potential solvent accessible volume in desolvated CuHIN is around $66.1\%$ as determined by the PLATON/SOLV program giving a calculated density of $0.995\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-3}$ for the desolvated framework.[11]
In order to investigate the effect of the packing mode on gas adsorption, the ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm was measured at $77~\mathsf{K}$ . As shown in Figure S5, the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ uptake amount is $362.2~\mathsf{c m}^{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (STP) at 1 bar (Figure 2a). The Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface area of CuHIN was calculated to be $834.3\ \mathsf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1},$ 2·g‒1, the Langmuir surface area of CuHIN was estimated to be $1020\ m^{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Furthermore, the pore volume given by HK equation is $0.55~{\mathsf{c m}}^{3}{\cdot}{\mathsf{g}}^{-1}.$ , close to the theoretical value of 0.664 cm3·g‒1 calculated using PLATON.[11]
Furthermore, the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capacities of CuHIN were further measured at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and $298\ \mathsf{K},$ respectively. As shown in Figure 3, CuHIN exhibits apparently higher ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capacity. At a relatively low pressure (0.15 bar, a typical partial pressure for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ in flue gases), the uptake amounts of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ are 44.8 and 24 $\mathsf{c m}^{3}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , corresponding to 9.3 and $4.7~\mathsf{w t\%}$ at 273 and $298\ \mathsf{K},$ respectively. Meanwhile, the uptake capacity of CuHIN is higher than that of traditional two-dimensional materials such as graphene and typical 2D-MOFs Qc-5-Cu-sql $3.4~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $298~\mathsf{K},~0.15$ bar), [12,7i] and as well higher than that of some typical 3D Cu-MOFs, such as Cu-BTTri (2.9 wt% at 298 K and 0.15 bar),[13] SNU-50 $12.9~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $298\mathsf{K},$ and 0.15 bar),[14] UMCM-150 ( $1.8~\mathsf{w t\%}$ at $298\textsf{K}$ and 0.15 bar),[15] and en- $\mathtt{\cdot c u}$ -BTTri $12.3~\mathsf{w t\%}$ at $298\textsf{K}$ and 0.15 bar).[13] Surely, the higher ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of CuHIN could be attributed to the dynamic process in the crystals of a 2D net containing AB packing mode. For a better interpretation of the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption mechanism, we further calculated their ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption enthalpies (Figure S9). The zero-coverage ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption enthalpy of CuHIN is $32k J\cdot m0l^{-1}$ . Without any open metal sites, the higher ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption enthalpy in CuHIN should be attributed to the suitable pore sizes, which are triggered by the dynamic process in the crystals of a 2D net containing AB packing mode and are more suitable for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ accommodation.
The selectivities for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C H}}_{4}$ of CuHIN at different temperatures were calculated by ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST) to evaluate its selective ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption from the experimental single-component isotherms based on $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ $(0.15:0.85)$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C H}_{4}$ (1 : 1) mixtures, which are 165.8 and 10.22 at $298\mathsf{K},$ respectively (Figure 4, Table S4), lower than 40000 and 3300 of
2D-MOF Qc-5-Cu-sql at $298~\mathsf{K},^{[7\mathsf{i}]}$ but higher than 36 and 7 of 2D-MOF Qc-5-Ni-dia, as well as 19 and 3 of 2D-MOF Qc-5-Cu-dia.[7i] Furthermore, these values are larger than those of typical 3D-MOFs SIFSIX-2-Cu-I $(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2},$ 140),[2a] $\mathsf{c u}$ -TDPAT $(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2},$ 79; ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C H}}_{4},$ 5.3),[16] ZIF-78 $(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2},50.1_{\cdot}$ ; ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C H}}_{4},$ 10.6),[17] and NJU-Bai33 $\mathsf{(C O_{2}/N_{2},}$ 40.3; ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C H}}_{4},$ 8.9) under similar conditions.[9e] The higher selectivity for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ of CuHIN might mainly result from its higher surface area and the AB packing mode through the $\pi^{\cdot\cdot\cdot\pi}$ interactions of the pyridine rings in the adjacent layers.
Through comparison of the factors that work between MOF-2 and CuHIN, we got to know that the layers of 2D MOFs are held together depending on the packing mode, which is composed by different interactions between the adjacent layers. To further understand the effect of packing mode on sorption behavior in 2D MOFs, we synthesized $[\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{H P B})_{4}]_{n}$ based upon 4-(4-pyridyl)-benzoic acid (HPB), which was elongated by phenyl. Based upon the reaction conditions of CuHIN, CuHPB was synthesized. Single X-ray crystal structure revealed that it crystallizes in monoclinic space group $c2/c$ . The composition of Cu-paddlewheel units in CuHPB is similar to that of CuHIN, also leading to the formation of 2D square topological layers (sql) with pore size of $8.9\times8.9\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{2}$ square grids (Figure 1d). These layers are held together along the $a$ axis by three interactions in the adjacent layers. Firstly, the $\pi^{\cdot\cdot\cdot\pi}$ interactions of the aromatic rings in the adjacent layers; secondly, the $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}\cdots\uppi$ interactions between acetic acid ligands of one layer and aromatic ring of another adjacent layer; Thirdly, the ${\mathsf{C}}{-}{\mathsf{H}}{\cdots}{\uppi}$ interactions between the solvent DMF and the adjacent layer. The two-dimensional layers are held together tightly along the $a$ axis by the three interactions, with a short interlayer separation of $3.5286\mathrm{\AA},$ giving an ABC packing mode of 3D framework (Figures 1a and 1c). Thereby, the layers could not slide on each other because of the fairly strong binding forces during them. Furthermore, in order to confirm the effect of the packing mode on gas adsorption, we measured the ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption of CuHPB at $77\upkappa$ . The BET surface areas of CuHPB is $4\bar{6}.1\mathsf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1},$ and the Langmuir surface area of CuHPB is estimated to be $95~\mathsf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (Figures S5 and S6). Clearly, by providing dynamic porous channel of the adjacent layers via fine-tuning of the packing mode of a 2D net through ligand elongation, the surface of these porous structure could be more difficult to adsorb gas molecules, and thus the expansion structure, CuHPB, shows lower surface area. Meanwhile, the $\mathsf{C O}_{2},$ $C H_{4}$ adsorption capacities of CuHPB were further measured at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and $298\ \mathsf{K},$ respectively (Figures S7 and S8). CuHPB exhibits relatively low $\mathsf{C O}_{2},$ $C H_{4}$ adsorption capacity. By comparing with CuHIN and other two-dimensional materials, we found a conclusion that, for the 2D MOFs, the dynamic porous structure depends on the interaction of the adjacent layers. However, the stronger interaction is not good for gliding smoothly and gas adsorption.
## Conclusions
In summary, by a strategy of fine-tuning of packing mode between the adjacent layer in a 2D net MOFs through ligand changing, two 2D sql-type MOFs CuHIN and CuHPB were successfully synthesized, and interestingly, CuHIN has highly selective ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake. Compared with the precursor MOF-2, the fine-tuning structure of CuHIN shows selective adsorption of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ over $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ and $C H_{4}$ with the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake amounts as high as $4.7~\mathsf{w t\%}$ at 0.15 bar and at $298\mathrm{~K},$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ selectivity towards ${\sf N}_{2}$ and $C H_{4}$ is 165.8 and 10.22 at 1 bar and at $298\mathsf{K},$ respectively. It may be an excellently potential candidate for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture, such as the post-combustion ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture and the purification of natural gases. In addition, for the 2D MOF, the dynamic porous structure depends on the interaction of the adjacent layers, and the suitable interaction is good for gliding smoothly and gas adsorption.
## Experimental
## Synthesis of MOFs
Synthesis of $[\mathsf{C u(L)}(\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{C O O H})]_{n}$ (CuHIN). A solution of $C u C l_{2}\cdot2H_{2}O$ $(27.3~\mathrm{mg},0.16~\mathrm{mmol})$ in $1\mathsf{m L}$ of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ and $0.04~\mathrm{mL}$ of acetic acid was mixed with HPB $(10~\mathrm{mg},0.05~\mathrm{mmol})$ in $2.0~\mathsf{m L}$ of $N,N^{\prime}$ -dimethylformamide. The mixture was sealed in a Pyrex tube and heated to ${120}^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $60\mathrm{~h~}$ . The green plate crystals obtained were filtered and washed with DMF. Yield $\approx80\%$ . Selected $1{\mathsf{R}}\ v:$ 3423, 3120, 3075, 3015, 2971, 2873, 2467, 1965, 1670, 1600, 1552, 1556, 1496, 1469, 1415, 1375, 1307, 1255, 1232, 1203, 1148, 1102, 1048, 1102, 1087, 1054, 1034, 1022, 991, 871, 846, 778, 700, $661~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ .
Synthesis of $[{\mathsf{C u}}_{2}(\mathsf{L})_{2}{\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{C O O H}}]_{n}$ (CuHPB). A solution of $C u C l_{2}\cdot2H_{2}O$ $(27.3~\mathrm{mg},~0.16~\mathrm{mmol})$ ) in $1~\mathsf{m L}$ of ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}$ and $0.4~\mathsf{m L}$ of acetic acid was mixed with HIN $(10~\mathrm{mg},0.08~\mathrm{mmol})$ in $2.0~\mathsf{m L}$ of $N,N^{\prime}$ -dimethylformamide. The mixture was sealed in a Pyrex tube and heated to ${120}^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $60\mathrm{~h~}$ . The green plate crystals obtained were filtered and washed with DMF. Yield $\approx80\%$ . Selected IR ν: 3432, 3121, 3011, 1639, 1562, 1496, 1437, 1317, 1225, 1210, 1148, 1149, 1061, 1016, 863, 764, $698~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ .
Sample activation. The as-synthesized sample of CuHIN and CuHPB was soaked in acetone for 3 d with MeOH refreshing every 8 h. Then, the MeOH-exchanged sample of CuHIN was activated at $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and under vacuum for $10\mathsf{h}$ . The MeOH-exchanged sample of CuHPB was activated under vacuum at $40^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $10\mathsf{h}$ .
## $\pmb{\times}$ -ray single crystal structure determination
Single-crystal X-ray diffraction data were measured on a Bruker Apex II CCD diffractometer at $150~\mathsf{K}$ using graphite monochromated ${\sf M o}/{\sf K}{\sf a}$ radiation $(\lambda=0.71073\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ). Data reduction was made with the Bruker SAINT program. The structures were solved by direct methods and refined with full-matrix least squares technique using the SHELXTL package.[18] Non-hydrogen atoms were refined with anisotropic displacement parameters during the final cycles. Organic hydrogen atoms were placed in calculated positions with isotropic displacement parameters set to $1.2\times\mathsf{U e q}$ of the attached atom. The unit cell includes a large region of disordered solvent molecules, which could not be modeled as discrete atomic sites. We employed PLATON/SQUEEZE to calculate the diffraction contribution of the solvent molecules, thereby, to produce a set of solvent-free diffraction intensities; structures were then refined again using the data generated.[11]
## Low pressure gas sorption measurements
Low-pressure adsorption isotherms of ${\sf N}_{2}$ $\mathsf{I}_{2}\ (99.999\%)$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ $(99.999\%)$ gases were performed on Quanta chrome Autosorb IQ-2 surface area and pore size analyzer. Before analysis, about $50~\mathsf{m g}$ samples were activated by using the “outgas” function of the surface area analyzer. For all isotherms, ultra-high purity He gas (UHP grade 5.0, $99.999\%$ purity) was used for the estimation of the free space (warm and cold), assuming that it is not adsorbed at any of the studied temperatures. The specific surface areas were determined using the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) and the Langmuir equation from the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ sorption data at $77~\mathsf{K}.$ When applying the BET theory, we made sure that our analysis satisfied the two consistency criteria as detailed by Walton and co-workers.[19] For the Langmuir surface area, data from the whole adsorption data were used (Figures S5 and S8).
## Estimation of the isosteric heats of gas adsorption
A virial-type expression comprising the temperature-independent parameters $a_{i}$ and $b_{j}$ was employed to calculate the enthalpies of adsorption for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ (at 273 and 298 K) on CuHIN and CuHPB.[20] In each case, the data were fitted using the equation:
$$
\ln P=\ln N+1/T\sum_{i=0}^{m}a_{i}N^{i}+\sum_{j=0}^{n}b_{j}N^{j}
$$
Here, $P$ is the pressure expressed in Torr, $N$ is the amount adsorbed in $\mathsf{m m o l}/\mathsf{g},T$ is the temperature in $\mathsf{K},a_{i}$ and $b_{j}$ are virial coefficients, and $m,n$ represent the number of coefficients required to adequately describe the isotherms ( $m$ and $\mathfrak{n}$ were gradually increased until the contribution of extra added $a$ and $b$ coefficients was deemed to be statistically insignificant towards the overall fit, and the average value of the squared deviations from the experimental values was minimized). The values of the virial coefficients $a_{0}$ through ${\pmb q}_{m}$ were then used to calculate the isosteric heat of adsorption using the following expression.
$$
Q_{\mathrm{st}}=-R\sum_{i=0}^{m}a_{i}N^{i}
$$
In Figure S9, $\boldsymbol{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ is the coverage-dependent isosteric heat of adsorption and $R$ is the universal gas constant. The heat of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ sorption for CuHIN and CuHPB in the manuscript is determined by using the sorption data measured in the pressure range from $_{0-1}$ bar (273 and 298 K), which is fitted by the virial-equation very well $(R^{2}>0.9999)$ .
## Prediction of the Gases Adsorption Selectivity by IAST
$$
q=\frac{q_{m,1}b_{1}p^{1/n_{1}}}{1+b_{1}p^{1/n_{1}}}+\frac{q_{m,2}b_{2}p^{1/n_{2}}}{1+b_{2}p^{1/n_{2}}}
$$
Here, $p$ is the pressure of the bulk gas at equilibrium with the adsorbed phase (kPa), $q$ is the adsorbed amount per mass of adsorbent $(\mathsf{m m o l}/\mathsf{g})$ , $q_{m,1}$ and $q_{m,2}$ are the saturation capacities of sites 1 and 2 $(\mathsf{m m o l}/\mathsf{g})$ , $b_{1}$ and $b_{2}$ are the affinity coefficients of sites 1 and 2 (1/kPa), and $n_{1}$ and $n_{2}$ represent the deviations from an ideal homogeneous surface. The fitted parameters were then used to predict multi-component adsorption with IAST.
The selectivity $S_{\mathsf{A/B}}$ in a binary mixture of components A and B is defined as $(x_{\mathbb{A}}/y_{\mathbb{A}})/(x_{\mathbb{B}}/y_{\mathbb{B}}).$ , where $x_{i}$ and $y_{i}$ are the mole fractions of component $i(i=A,{\mathsf{B}})$ in the adsorbed and bulk phases, respectively.
